ls  one  and since we want to apply this to both  of our y axes this seems like  the logical way to do it  but when we look at the plot something  is not quite right  the rotation has taken effect but the  justification has not so only on the  left hand side  are our axis labels right justified  on the right hand side they don't match  so why would why would one of these  changes  take effect but not the other one and  the reason for this has to do with the  base theme that i'm using  so i built the theme for these plots on  the default ggplot theme  theme grey and if we printed out the  whole theme it would be  like super long so so we're not going to  look at all of it but i've just put on  the slide  the the parts of it that have to do with  axis text  you can see that theme gray sets the  margins and the justification  of axis text y and then it actually  adjusts the  adjusts those details for axis text  y right and in particular theme grey is  setting  the justification of text in axis text y  right to zero corresponding to a left  justified  text this brings us to  the crucial piece of the theme system  which is that when you're customizing  theme elements  the most specific element wins if the  theme that you're using  sets a specific element like the  justification  of axis text y right then if you want to  change it  you need to edit that very specific  element and not any of the ones above it  so in our case theme gray sets the  justification but not the angle  of axis text y so when we  change axis text y the justification  that we provided  gets overridden by the theme that we're  using  this is something that has thrown off so  many people it's thrown off me  it's thrown off other contributors to  ggplot  it's it comes up all of the time because  it is a subtle and slightly confusing  aspect of  of how this theme inheritance  inheritance works  but when you think about the fact that  it's working by specificity  and not by the order in which you  uh pro